WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.588
Response policy zones are BIND's filter.

2
00:00:02.588 --> 00:00:06.333
They're ordinary DNS zones,
up to 64 per server,

3
00:00:06.333 --> 00:00:10.687
whose triggers map to actions
like NXDOMAIN or drop.

4
00:00:10.687 --> 00:00:13.201
They stay in sync through zone transfers.

5
00:00:13.201 --> 00:00:18.250
CISA's Protective DNS Resolver is
mandatory for civilian agencies.

6
00:00:18.250 --> 00:00:23.323
CISA says agency DNS should use only
Protective DNS as its upstream,

7
00:00:23.323 --> 00:00:29.375
and cloud infrastructure should use
Protective DNS or agency DNS as its upstream.

8
00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:32.682
CISA's pages don't mention
response policy zones,

9
00:00:32.682 --> 00:00:35.425
so the layering is NIST's
and the program's.

10
00:00:35.425 --> 00:00:40.270
As design guidance: the agency's
own policy zones run on cloud BIND,

11
00:00:40.270 --> 00:00:44.038
from the same feed as on
premises, in front of CISA's.

12
00:00:44.038 --> 00:00:48.221
Internet-bound queries leave
only toward Protective DNS.

13
00:00:48.221 --> 00:00:50.226
Hits and blocks go to the SOC.

14
00:00:50.226 --> 00:00:54.936
And BIND 9.20 can forward
over DNS over TLS;

15
00:00:54.936 --> 00:00:59.980
CISA's 2024 note that it
can't was true of 9.18.
